[22.733137, "o", "- The jq arguments and filter must be saved to '\"'\"'/app/jq_args.yaml'\"'\"' as specified in the instructions above.  '; tmux wait -S done\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[183.490822, "o", "case \"$FILTER\" in *'\\'*) echo \"ABORT: filter still contains a backslash\"; exit 1;; esac\r\nprintf 'jq --indent 2 %s /app/users.json > /app/active_users_report.json\\n' \"'$FILTER'\" > cmd.sh\r\n{ printf \"filter: '%s'\\n\" \"$FILTER\"; printf 'args:\\n  - \"--indent 2\"\\n'; } > jq_args.yaml\r\necho \"--- cmd.sh:\"; cat cmd.sh\r\necho \"--- jq_args.yaml:\"; cat jq_args.yaml\r\nY=$(yq -r '.filter' jq_args.yaml)\r\n[ \"$Y\" = \"$FILTER\" ] && echo \"PASS: filter round-trips through yq byte-identically\" || { echo \"FAIL: round-trip mismatch\"; exit 1; }\r\nEOS\r\nsh /tmp/mk.sh — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && cat > /tmp/mk.sh <<'EOS' cd /app FILTER='[ .[] | select(.st…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Both files now match and carry no shell/YAML-escaping hazards. Final full…\r\n[approval] bash: cd /app && rm -f active_users_report.json && sh cmd.sh && echo \"cmd.sh exit=$?\" && sh /tmp/verify.sh; echo \"VERIFY_EXIT=$?\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && rm -f active_users_report.json && sh cmd.sh &&"]
[183.491, "o", " echo \"cmd.sh…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ All 30 checks pass. Final look at the artifacts and a sample of the repor…\r\n[approval] bash: cd /app && ls -l active_users_report.json cmd.sh jq_args.yaml && echo \"=== report: first 2 + last 2 records, plus an empty-roles example:\" && jq -c '.[0], .[1]' active_users_report.json && jq -c '[.[]|select(.role_count==0)][0]' active_users_report.json && jq -c '.[-1]' active_users_report.json && echo \"=== counts:\" && jq '{total:length, with_roles:([.[]|select(.role_count>0)]|length), empty_roles:([.[]|select(.role_count==0)]|length)}' active_users_report.json — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /app && ls -l active_users_report.json cmd.sh jq_args.yaml && echo …\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 21 tool call(s) so far\r\nDone. All three artifacts are on disk at `/app/` and verified by running them.\r\n\r\n**Results**\r\n\r\n- `/app/active_users_report.json` — 346 users (of 500), sorted by `username`, 2-space inde"]
[183.491026, "o", "nted, trailing newline. 296 have a primary role; 50 have `role_count: 0` / `primary_role: null`.\r\n- `/app/cmd.sh` — the exact executed command (jq + `--indent 2` + filter + fixed input + redirection).\r\n- `/app/jq_args.yaml` — `filter` (string) and `args` (`[\"--indent 2\"]`), which reconstruct the same command byte-for-byte via the spec's `jq $(yq -r '.args[]' …) \"$(yq -r '.filter' …)\"` form.\r\n\r\n**Data facts I checked rather than assumed:** statuses are `active`/`inactive`/`pending` (so filtering on `\"active\"` is required; 154 non-active rows excluded); `roles` is always an array and may be empty; all 500 `last_login` values are 27-char fractional-second UTC timestamps. jq 1.6's `fromdateiso8601` rejects fractional seconds, so the filter truncates to `[0:19]` + `\"Z\"` before parsing, and `gmtime` is used so the date can't shift with the host timezone.\r\n\r\n**Two things worth flagging from the build:**\r\n\r\n1. Your `jq_args.yaml` reconstruction depends on `yq`, and the installed one is kislyuk's Python `yq 3."]
[183.491049, "o", "1.0` (reports `--version` as `0.0.0`). I tested the actual reconstruction rather than assuming, and confirmed it produces a byte-identical file.\r\n2. My first attempt at the filter used `sub(\"\\\\.[0-9]+Z$\"; \"Z\")`. Shell escaping silently reduced `\\\\` to `\\` in the YAML, which would have made jq reject the filter — the file would have looked fine while failing at grading time. I removed all backslashes from the filter, so neither `cmd.sh` nor the YAML has an escaping dependency, and I asserted the filter round-trips through `yq -r '.filter'` unchanged.\r\n\r\nThe verifier (`/tmp/verify.sh`, outside the repo, so it isn't left behind) recomputes every expectation from `users.json` and cross-checks each output record against its source by `id`: field names/order, date format, role counts, null handling for empty roles, sort order, exact indentation, absence of tabs, and byte-equality of both the `cmd.sh` run and the spec reconstruction. Re-running it now exits 0 with all 30 checks passing.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implement"]
